 out of  sync  but unfortunately this is best  documented in shift workers where  chronic circadian disruption has put  them at increased risk of chronic  diseases associated with aging  but with these studies there are many  confounding factors such as different  sleep and activity levels throughout the  day  as it's not just the timing of feed  intake that's changing her  so as I already mentioned this phrases  the question as to whether there is an  optimal time to eat  well Sachin panzo is one of the many  researchers now who are interested in  addressing this question  it's been a while now since I read his  book the Circadian codes but that was  where I first learned about his research  on time restricted feeding while you  limit food intake to a specific time  window of 9 to 12 hours  having isocaloric time restricted  feeding studies in rodents have  suggested improvements in several  metabolic parameters including glucose  and Insulin homeostasis energy  production liver pathology and  resistance to different abusogenic diets  there was also a paper that showed that  that the time restructured feeding  pattern extended lifespan and improved  age-related Health outcomes in mice but  the study I think also had an impact on  the amount of food that the mice were  taking so can't really distinguish  between the two  and as with this last time I'll study  some human Studies have been done but  it's also impacted calorie intake  sort of appears to be some sort of  scientific backing to the importance of  when food is eaten but there hasn't been  that many studies that have directly  compared within the same group how  adjusting eating windows from early to  late changes different biomarkers  many epidemiological Studies have  Associated skipping breakfast with  increased risk for cardiovascular  disease cancer and overall mortality  but again there are just many  confounding variables and we can't  really take too much from these  epidemiological studies  and so 