rning loaded diet  versus an evening loaded diet so the  timings were the same it's just they ate  more of their calories either in the  morning or the evening as you can see  here  and so the individual calorie intake was  fixed  and at least according to the authors  since all food and beverages were  provided it made it the most rigorously  controlled study to assess timing of  eating in humans to date with the aim of  accounting for all aspects of energy  balance  and so their main finding was that  morning load energy intake resulted in  lower daily subjective appetite and  hunger matching the first study but here  they didn't see any difference in energy  metabolism between the morning loaded  versus the evening loaders  they also didn't see any significant  differences between the two groups when  they looked at changes in glucose  insulin and blood lipid profiles  I say the reason that I mentioned these  last sort of measurements is that this  brings us on to the third study  randomized control trial for  time-restricted eating in healthy  volunteers without obesity  whether somebody looked at healthy  individuals over a five-week randomized  trial comparing early versus mid-time  restricted feeding the early time  restricted feeding including an eight  hour period between 6 a.m and 3pm whilst  the mid had a eight hour window between  11AM and 8pm  and then they were comparing the data to  a free eating control group  these are just some of the results on  energy intake and metabolic health  related parameters and just focusing on  early versus mids you can see how  there's a slightly lower energy intake  with the early group but the only other  significant change was in this Interline  resistance index that is calculated  based on fasting glucose and insulin and  concentrations but there was an  improvement in the early eating group  and for each of these data points this  is the change over five weeks  so Benjamin Franklin is famous for  saying to lengthen